<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tolfa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tolfa is a hill town in the Tolfa Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The discovery of rich alum deposits there in the early 1460s gave the Papal States a lucrative monopoly of a mineral vital to the European cloth industry, replacing supplies lost when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a>. The alum was mined for centuries under papal concession, for a time by the Medici bank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tolfa.jpg" alt="Tolfa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pippo-b · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tolfa_05-080325.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
